Here's the full audit:

## Time-Related Static Dependencies (7 total)

### `Services/OrderProcessor.cs` — 4 hits
| Line | Call |
|------|------|
| 21 | `DateTime.UtcNow` (order creation) |
| 22 | `DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays(30)` (expiry calc) |
| 25 | `DateTime.Now` (receipt string) |
| 34 | `DateTime.UtcNow` (expiry check) |

### `Services/NotificationService.cs` — 3 hits
| Line | Call |
|------|------|
| 7 | `DateTime.UtcNow` (days-until-expiry calc) |
| 16 | `DateTime.UtcNow` (log filename) |
| 18 | `DateTime.UtcNow` (log timestamp) |

---

## Recommendation: Use `TimeProvider` (.NET 8+)

.NET 8 introduced the abstract `TimeProvider` class — inject it via constructor:

```csharp
public class OrderProcessor(TimeProvider clock)
{
    public Order CreateOrder(...)
    {
        var now = clock.GetUtcNow();
        // ...
    }
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var processor = new OrderProcessor(fake);
// advance time deterministically
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(31));
Assert.True(processor.IsExpired(order));
```

In production DI registration:

```csharp
services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

This eliminates all 7 static calls, makes every time-dependent path fully deterministic in tests, and avoids the `DateTime.Now` vs `UtcNow` inconsistency on line 25. Would you like me to apply this refactoring?
